# CPython 3.14t OPTIONAL feature patch: thread-state EXECUTION home
#   build flag:  -DPy_TSTATE_EXEC_HOME      (OFF by default)
#   pinned to:   Python-3.14.4  (applies at ZERO fuzz: patch -p1 -F0)
#   series pair: this is the EXECUTION half for the 3.14 series.  The matching
#                ALLOCATION half is cpython314t-tstate-alloc-home.patch;
#                migration needs BOTH.
#
# WHAT: stops the compiler caching "which OS thread is this frame running on".
# Two reads carry that identity and both are covered:
#   _PyThreadState_GET()  -> out-of-line call  (pycore_pystate.h, pystate.c)
#   _Py_ThreadId()        -> volatile asm      (object.h)
# Plus two buffer-export counts, which a migration makes a fiber's own
# deallocs race (see ALSO below):
#   _PyManagedBufferObject.exports -> atomic ++/--  (memoryobject.c)
#   PyMemoryViewObject.exports     -> atomic in hex()/hash() too (3.14 backport)
#   arrayobject.ob_exports         -> atomic ++/--  (arraymodule.c)
# Plus one lock owner, which a migration changes and fibers on one OS thread
# share (see ALSO below):
#   _PyRecursiveMutex.thread (_thread.RLock, the import and warnings locks)
#                         -> the attached thread state's id, not the OS thread
#                                                         (lock.c, import.c)
# Plus the waits on locks held across Python code, which a fiber runtime may
# redirect so a lock's waiter parks the FIBER instead of its OS thread (see
# ALSO below):
#   _thread.lock / RLock waits
#                         -> _Py_fiber_park hooks  (parking_lot.c,
#                            _threadmodule.c, pycore_semaphore.h)
#
# WHY: compilers treat thread identity as loop-invariant and hoist/CSE it.  That
# is sound for OS threads, whose stacks never move.  A stackful fiber parks on
# one OS thread and resumes on another carrying its C frames, so those cached
# values keep naming the ORIGIN thread.  Measured, clang -O2, reading twice
# around an opaque call that may migrate the fiber:
#
#   Darwin/AArch64    caches the ADDRESS -- one tlv_get_addr per function,
#                     spilled to the stack frame and reused.
#   Linux x86-64      caches the VALUE -- second read deleted, pre-call value
#   and AArch64       kept in a callee-saved register.  Same under
#                     -ftls-model=local-exec / initial-exec / global-dynamic.
#   _Py_ThreadId()    elided on ALL THREE: both reads deleted and
#                     `tid_before == tid_after` folded to `mov w0, #1`.
#
# So in a migrated frame _PyThreadState_GET() resolves against the origin thread:
# NULL if it has detached -> SIGSEGV (_Py_Dealloc -> _Py_RecursionLimit_GetMargin
# at offset 0x350, _PyCriticalSection_BeginMutex 0xb0, _PyErr_Occurred 0x70), or
# a valid-but-WRONG tstate if it is running -> silent corruption.
#
# This is the EXECUTION half of migration support; the ALLOCATION half is
# cpython314t-tstate-alloc-home.patch.  They are independent and both are needed:
# alloc-home alone still segfaults at H>=2 under migration.
#
# MEASURED (stackweave, CPython 3.14.4t, arm64 Darwin, release -O2; three
# interpreters built identically apart from the patch; repro
# tests/experiments/resume_rebuild/exec_home_min.py at 4 hubs, 10 runs each):
#
#   alloc-home only                  SIGSEGV 10/10
#   + _PyThreadState_GET() half      clean   10/10   <- the crash is THIS half
#   + both halves                    clean   10/10
#
# COST, measured against the alloc-home-only build (i.e. the marginal cost of
# THIS patch, not of migration support as a whole).  Interpreter-bound
# microbenchmark, 2M iters, min of 5:
#
#                     alloc-home   +this patch
#   dict alloc churn    0.152s       0.176s   (+16%)
#   function call       0.042s       0.045s   (+7%)
#   list alloc churn    0.112s       0.124s   (+11%)
#
# All of it is the _PyThreadState_GET() out-of-lining: the half-only build
# measures 0.175/0.044/0.125, indistinguishable from both halves.  The volatile
# _Py_ThreadId() half is free.
#
# NOT DEMONSTRATED: no failure has been pinned on the _Py_ThreadId() half -- the
# SIGSEGV above is entirely the other one.  Its biased-refcount mechanism is read
# off refcount.h, and a lost refcount update is silent, so a clean run cannot
# distinguish "unnecessary" from "not yet observed".  Kept because it is free.
# Settling it needs ThreadSanitizer on ob_ref_local under migration; not run.
#
# NOT RUN ON LINUX: the Linux codegen above is cross-compiled evidence only;
# every runtime result here is arm64 Darwin.
#
# ALSO -- a dealloc that runs on the far side of a migration (memoryobject.c,
# arraymodule.c).
# Biased refcounting sends a non-owner's last decref to the OS thread that owns
# the object (ob_tid, set where it was allocated), and that thread's merge runs
# tp_dealloc.  For a fiber that has moved, the owner is the hub it ALLOCATED on,
# so the dealloc of an object it never shared runs concurrently with its own next
# statements on the new hub.  Code that a dealloc and its creator both touch
# must therefore be thread-safe even for single-fiber programs.  CPython has an
# OPEN CLASS of export counters that are not -- a plain ++/-- in a getbuffer /
# releasebuffer pair or a view's register/release -- and these hunks fix two
# members: memoryview's (below) and array.array's (after it).  bytearray's is
# under the object's critical section; the rest of the class is not audited.
#
# The memoryview instance: _PyManagedBufferObject.exports, bumped by every view
# of a buffer on creation (mbuf_add_*view) and dropped on release/dealloc
# (_memory_release), is a plain ++/-- upstream (still on main).  The per-view
# PyMemoryViewObject.exports is updated atomically by memory_getbuf /
# memory_releasebuf, but 3.14.4's memoryview.hex() and memory_hash() still
# bump it with a plain ++/--; the hunk backports the 3.14 branch's
# FT_ATOMIC_ADD_SSIZE there (3.15 already has it).
# A fiber holding `sl = view[0:]` across a park and dropping it on another hub
# has the old hub run `--exports` while it runs `exports++` for the next slice;
# a lost increment releases the buffer under the live `view` ("operation
# forbidden on released memoryview object"; the exporter is unpinned and may
# resize/free the memory the view points at), a lost decrement pins the
# exporter forever.  The objects' refcounts stay exact throughout -- only the
# export count is lost.  Upstream bug as well: plain threads slicing one shared
# view release it the same way on a stock 3.14.4t; migration just turns a
# shared-object race into a private-object one.  Measured, 64 ping-pong pairs
# each slicing its own view across a park, batches to ~3000 OS-thread moves
# per run, two interpreters built identically apart from this hunk (arm64
# Darwin, no LTO/PGO; "with" held at 0 over 10 such runs):
#
#                 H=2        H=4        H=8        H=12
#   without     179 / 283   60 / 114   43 / 93    44 / 117   (released views /
#   with          0 / 0      0 / 0      0 / 0      0 / 0      leaked counts)
#
# Guard: tests/test_cross_hub_migration.py::
#        test_memory_memoryview_slice_survives_a_migration.
# memoryobject.c is not installed, so the object.h hunk also defines
# _Py_MV_EXPORTS_ATOMIC (under the flag) as an installed witness: on an
# interpreter patched with an older copy the guard still runs, but a failure
# there is an xfail asking for a rebuild.
#
# The array.array instance: ob_exports is bumped by array_buffer_getbuf and
# dropped by array_buffer_relbuf with no critical section (still on main;
# python/cpython#157759, filed under gh-154524, proposes the same fix).  A fiber
# holding `memoryview(arr)` across a park and dropping it on another hub has
# the old hub run the release while it takes the next view.  A lost decrement
# pins the array ("cannot resize an array that is exporting buffers"), a lost
# increment lets it resize under a live view.  The hunk makes the count atomic
# under the flag (the three "is it exporting?" checks read it with acquire,
# so a resize is ordered after the release it saw).
# Measured, 64 ping-pong pairs each holding a view of its own array across a
# park, batches to ~3000 OS-thread moves per run at H=8 (arm64 Darwin, no
# LTO/PGO): without it 186-261 of 2112-2688 arrays lost an update per run
# (149-215 pinned, 37-49 resizable under a live view); with it, none over 10 runs.
# Guard: tests/test_cross_hub_migration.py::
#        test_memory_array_view_survives_a_migration.  arraymodule.c is not
# installed either; its witness is _Py_ARRAY_EXPORTS_ATOMIC in object.h.
#
# ALSO -- a lock owned by the OS thread, waited for by the OS thread (lock.c,
# parking_lot.c, _threadmodule.c, import.c).  A fiber that parks inside a lock
# and resumes on another OS thread must still own it, and the other fibers on
# its old OS thread must not: so a _PyRecursiveMutex's owner is the attached
# thread state (_PyRecursiveMutex_SelfIdent(): tstate->id, tagged odd so it
# never equals an OS thread id).  And a waiter must not block an OS thread a
# parked holder may need: the parking lot asks _Py_fiber_park (installed by
# the fiber runtime) to park the waiting FIBER for the _thread lock objects'
# waits.  Everything else blocks the thread as before.  Full story,
# measurements and guards: src/patches/README.md, "A lock owned by the OS
# thread, waited for by the OS thread".
#
# ZERO IMPACT BY DEFAULT: with the flag undefined every site macro-expands back
# to stock.  No public ABI change: the fields it adds are internal
# (_PySemaphore).
#
# CAVEATS
#   * Do NOT build with --with-lto (or -flto): LTO inlines pystate.c's direct
#     _Py_tss_tstate readers and writers (PyThreadState_Get(),
#     _PyThreadState_Attach/Detach, PyEval_SaveThread, ...) into ~400 callers
#     across the interpreter, each then holding a cacheable slot address.
#     Py_NO_INLINE keeps _PyThreadState_GetCurrent() itself out of line even
#     under LTO; it is the other accessors that leak.  PGO alone
#     (--enable-optimizations) does no cross-TU inlining and is safe.  Check a
#     build with tools/ci/check_exec_home_tls.py (src/patches/README.md,
#     "Build flags").
#   * Do NOT annotate _PyThreadState_GetCurrent() pure/const -- it looks eligible
#     and would silently re-license the folding this prevents.
#   * APPLY EVERY HUNK.  Py_TSTATE_EXEC_HOME is armed in pyconfig.h independently
#     of whether the patch landed, so a partial build would advertise the feature
#     while lacking it.  The object.h hunk defines _Py_TID_ASM in both arms as a
#     compile-time witness; test for both (stackweave does).  Check by hand with:
#         grep _Py_TID_ASM Include/object.h
#         grep _Py_MV_EXPORTS_ATOMIC Include/object.h
#         grep MBUF_EXPORTS_INC Objects/memoryobject.c
#         grep _Py_ARRAY_EXPORTS_ATOMIC Include/object.h
#         grep ARRAY_EXPORTS_INC Modules/arraymodule.c
#         grep _Py_RMUTEX_TSTATE_IDENT Include/object.h
#         grep _Py_TSTATE_FIBER_FLAG Include/internal/pycore_tstate.h
#         grep recursive_mutex_self Python/lock.c
#         grep _Py_FIBER_PARK_HOOKS Include/object.h
#         grep _Py_fiber_park Python/parking_lot.c
#   * MSVC thread-id reads are intrinsics, not asm, and are left untouched.
#   * Same class, not covered (not read by a fiber across a park in any path
#     exercised so far): import.c's `pkgcontext`, mimalloc's _mi_heap_default.
#   * The lock-owner identity is a FIBER's thread state only: one a fiber
#     runtime marked (_PyThreadStateImpl._fiber, pycore_tstate.h, set before its
#     first attach).  Every other state keeps the OS thread, so an interpreter
#     that never runs a fiber is stock -- a C library's thread may take an
#     RLock in one PyGILState callback and release it in the next, on a fresh
#     state.  The fiber hooks get the parked-on address and the _Py_PARK_* bits
#     (_Py_PARK_INTR: the wait handles signals, so the runtime may return
#     Py_PARK_INTR to let the fiber run the handler, as the thread would).
#
# ENABLE: ./configure --disable-gil CPPFLAGS=-DPy_TSTATE_EXEC_HOME
#         (also add the #define to pyconfig.h so extension modules agree)
# APPLY:  from the CPython source root:  patch -p1 -F0 < this-file
# SCOPE:  Include/object.h, Include/internal/pycore_pystate.h, Python/pystate.c,
#         Include/internal/pycore_tstate.h,
#         Objects/memoryobject.c, Modules/arraymodule.c, Python/lock.c,
#         Include/internal/pycore_lock.h, Python/import.c, Python/parking_lot.c,
#         Include/internal/pycore_semaphore.h, Include/internal/pycore_parking_lot.h,
#         Modules/_threadmodule.c, Modules/_testinternalcapi/test_lock.c
#
